# Games, each played far enough in to show a board, a hand or a room
# rather than a title screen.  One stanza per program; every stanza makes
# whatever it needs for itself.

# ---------------------------------------------------------------- adventure

shot    advcom
size    30 80
cap     The ADVSYS compiler: it turns an adventure written in ADVSYS source
cap     into the world file the interpreter plays, naming every actor,
cap     location and thing as it goes and counting the words, objects and
cap     code at the end.  The sample adventure is in GAMES/ADVSYS.  advcom
cap     opens its `@objects.adi' include by bare name in the data directory
cap     and keeps a filename in twenty characters, so both files are copied
cap     to a working directory and it is run there, under a shell whose
cap     `chd' moves the data directory.
try     advcom osample
run     mkdir -p /dd/tmp/GAMESA > /nil 2>/nil
wait    3
run     cat /dd/GAMES/ADVSYS/osample.adv > /dd/tmp/GAMESA/osample.adv
wait    4
run     cat /dd/GAMES/ADVSYS/objects.adi > /dd/tmp/GAMESA/objects.adi
wait    4
run     load /dd/CMDS/GAMES/advcom
wait    4
run     clear
wait    1
run     sh -c "chd /dd/tmp/GAMESA; advcom osample"
wait    45

shot    advint
size    24 80
cap     The ADVSYS interpreter, playing the world advcom has just built from
cap     the sample source: you start in the livingroom, `n' takes you to the
cap     hallway and `e' into the storage room, where there is a rusty key.
cap     Type at the `:' prompt.  Like the compiler it opens its world file by
cap     bare name, so it is run in the directory holding it.
try     advint osample
run     mkdir -p /dd/tmp/GAMESA > /nil 2>/nil
wait    3
run     cat /dd/GAMES/ADVSYS/osample.adv > /dd/tmp/GAMESA/osample.adv
wait    4
run     cat /dd/GAMES/ADVSYS/objects.adi > /dd/tmp/GAMESA/objects.adi
wait    4
run     load /dd/CMDS/GAMES/advcom
wait    3
run     sh -c "chd /dd/tmp/GAMESA; advcom osample" > /nil
wait    45
run     load /dd/CMDS/GAMES/advint
wait    3
run     clear
wait    1
run     sh -c "chd /dd/tmp/GAMESA; advint osample"
wait    12
send    look\r
wait    5
send    n\r
wait    5
send    e\r
wait    6

shot    advent
cap     Colossal Cave, the first adventure of them all.  It loads its twelve
cap     sections of text, offers instructions, and drops you on the road
cap     outside the brick building; `in' takes you to the well house, where
cap     the lamp and the keys are, and `xyzzy' is the magic word.  Its world
cap     file lives in GAMES/adv.
try     advent
run     advent
wait    8
send    no\r
wait    5
send    in\r
wait    5
send    take lamp\r
wait    5
send    take keys\r
wait    5
send    xyzzy\r
wait    6

shot    infocom.tcap
cap     An interpreter for Infocom's Z-machine, playing a story file: a
cap     status line across the top with the room name and the score, the
cap     text below it, and a `>' prompt for commands like `look' and
cap     `inventory'.  Three Inform demonstration stories ship in
cap     GAMES/INFORM -- dejavu, hellow and shell.  This build reads the
cap     terminal description from termcap, and is the one to use at a
cap     terminal.
try     infocom.tcap dejavu.z3
run     builtin cd /dd/GAMES/INFORM
wait    1
run     clear
wait    1
run     infocom.tcap dejavu.z3
wait    8
send    \r
wait    4
send    look\r
wait    5
send    \r
wait    4
send    inventory\r
wait    6

shot    infocom
cap     The same Z-machine interpreter without the termcap display code.  It
cap     plays the story, but it writes the cursor codes for its status line
cap     as literal text, which is the row of brackets down the left.  For a
cap     readable screen use infocom.tcap; RETURN clears each `More' prompt.
try     infocom dejavu.z3
run     builtin cd /dd/GAMES/INFORM
wait    1
run     clear
wait    1
run     infocom dejavu.z3
wait    8
send    \r
wait    5
send    \r
wait    5
send    look\r
wait    6

shot    paranoia
cap     The Paranoia text adventure.  It rolls you a Troubleshooter --
cap     attributes, secret society, mutant power, equipment -- and then
cap     wakes you on the floor of a briefing room.  RETURN clears each
cap     `More' and goes on; you get six clones.
try     paranoia
run     paranoia
wait    8
send    \r
wait    4
send    \r
wait    4
send    \r
wait    5

# ------------------------------------------------------------------- arcade

shot    lander
cap     A lunar lander flown over a scrolling landscape.  Space starts a
cap     descent, a digit sets the engine power, `x' or `k' fires the main
cap     thruster and `z'/`j' and `c'/`l' the side retros; the numbers on the
cap     pads are what each is worth.  The top line is altitude, position and
cap     the two rates of drift, the bottom line thrust and fuel.  Its score
cap     table is GAMES/lander.hs.
try     lander
run     lander
wait    8
send    \s
wait    4
send    5
wait    3
send    x
wait    4
snap
send    q
wait    2

shot    pacman
size    26 80
cap     Pac-Man in an ASCII maze: you are `OS9', chased by four ghosts named
cap     for old operating systems, and the keypad moves you -- 8 up, 2 down,
cap     4 left, 6 right.  It asks your name and whether you want instructions,
cap     then draws the board with TeleVideo cursor codes, which this terminal
cap     does not paint, so little of it reaches the screen.  Its maze, help
cap     and score files are in GAMES/pacman.
try     pacman
run     clear
run     pacman
send    Tester\r
wait    3
send    y
wait    4
snap
send    \s
wait    2
send    q
wait    2

shot    robots
size    24 80
cap     Robots: you are the `I', the robots are the `#', and they walk
cap     straight at you every time you move -- steer them into each other
cap     and they become junk heaps, `@'.  Run it with `-m' so they take one
cap     step per move of yours; the keys are the numeric keypad 1-9 with 5
cap     to stand still, `t' to teleport and `s' for a last stand.  The
cap     panel keeps the screen, robot and score counts; when they get you
cap     the top ten appears in the arena, under the name USER gives, and
cap     `robots -s' prints that list on its own.
try     robots -m
run     robots -m
wait    8
keys    8
wait    3
snap
keys    4
wait    3
snap
send    \003
wait    2

shot    snake
cap     Snake: you are the `I', the money is the `$' and the snake is the
cap     line of `s' with `S' for its head.  Move with h, j, k and l and
cap     reach the money before the snake reaches you; `x' quits.  Some of
cap     its cursor moves arrive as text, so the board picks up stray
cap     characters as you play.
try     snake
run     snake
wait    10
snap
send    x
wait    3

shot    worm
size    24 80
cap     Worm: you are the `@' and your body is the `o's.  h, j, k and l
cap     steer; with no key the worm keeps going the way it last went, and
cap     H, J, K and L run several squares at once.  Eat a digit to grow
cap     that much longer.  The game ends when the worm runs into the wall
cap     or into itself; control-C leaves.
try     worm
run     worm
wait    3
send    k
wait    2
send    l
wait    3
send    j
wait    2
send    L
wait    3
snap
send    \003
wait    2

shot    sokoban
cap     Sokoban: push every packet, the `$', onto a storage square, the
cap     `.', without trapping one in a corner.  `@' is you.  The status line
cap     counts the level, the packets, the ones stored and your moves and
cap     pushes.  Its fifty levels, its help text and its saved games are in
cap     GAMES/SOKOBAN, and it asks the system for your user name, so run it
cap     from a login.
try     sokoban
run     sokoban
wait    10
snap
send    \003
wait    3

shot    tet
cap     Tetris, with the well on the left and the menu on the right: `p'
cap     plays, s or j moves the falling piece left, f or l right, d or k
cap     turns it, space drops it, ESC pauses and `q' leaves to the
cap     high-score table it keeps in GAMES/tet.hs.  It wants a real
cap     terminal; from a pipe it draws the board and reads nothing.
try     tet
run     tet
wait    8
keys    p
wait    5
send    \s
wait    3
send    \s
wait    3
send    \s
wait    4
snap
send    q
wait    3

shot    wanderer
cap     Wanderer, a Boulderdash: `<|>' is you, the dots are earth you can
cap     dig through and the diamonds are what you are after -- forty-five on
cap     the first screen, with a thousand moves to get them.  Its thirty
cap     screens are in GAMES/WAND.
try     wanderer
run     wanderer
wait    10
snap
send    q
wait    3

# -------------------------------------------------------------- board & card

shot    back
size    40 80
burst
cap     Backgammon drawn as a full board, points numbered 1 to 24, with the
cap     dice cup and the doubling status on the right.  Single letters are
cap     the commands: `R' rolls, `D' doubles, `H' is the help, `N' starts a
cap     new game and `Q' quits.
try     back
run     export TERM=vt100
run     clear
run     back

shot    blackjack
size    24 80
cap     Las Vegas blackjack in BASIC09, one hand in: it
cap     asks your name and whether you want the rules, then takes a wager
cap     and deals.  RETURN draws a card, `s' stands, `d' doubles down, `x'
cap     splits a pair; a wager of 0 ends the game.  It runs through
cap     Microware's runb -- `blackjack' at your own OS-9 shell, `runb
cap     blackjack' here.
try     runb blackjack
run     load /h1/CMDS/runb
wait    2
run     clear
wait    1
run     runb blackjack
wait    4
send    Tester\r
wait    3
send    n\r
wait    3
send    25\r
wait    4
send    s\r
wait    6

shot    blackjak
cap     Las Vegas blackjack, with a hand dealt and a bet on the table: it
cap     gives you a stake of chips, asks what you want to bet, deals two cards
cap     against the dealer's one, and then asks whether to hit or stick.
cap     It wants the word `no', not `N'.  One of four games written in
cap     SNOBOL4; their data is in GAMES/SNOBOL.
try     blackjak
run     blackjak
wait    6
send    no\r
wait    5
send    10\r
wait    8

shot    chess
cap     Chess against the machine on a shaded board, one move in: it asks
cap     which colour you want, your name and whether the game is long or
cap     short, then takes a move as two squares -- `e2' for the piece and
cap     `e4' for where it goes, two keystrokes each with no RETURN.  Its
cap     reply is on the line under the board.
try     chess
run     chess
wait    8
keys    w
wait    4
send    Tester\r
wait    4
keys    s
wait    6
keys    e2
wait    5
keys    e4
wait    25
snap
send    \003
wait    3

shot    crib
cap     Cribbage: your hand fanned out on the left, the two pegging tracks
cap     on the right, and the cut that decides whose crib it is.  Discard by
cap     naming a card -- `7H' for the seven of hearts.  It needs TERM set,
cap     so run it from a login session.  Its instructions are in
cap     GAMES/CRIB.
try     crib
run     crib
wait    8
send    n\r
wait    4
send    S\r
wait    8
snap
send    \003
wait    3

shot    cribbage
cap     A second cribbage, drawn the same way but a different program: the
cap     board on the right, the dialogue under it, and the same cut for the
cap     crib.  It offers you the rules first.  Like `crib' it needs TERM.
try     cribbage
run     cribbage
wait    8
send    n\r
wait    4
send    s\r
wait    8
snap
send    \003
wait    3

shot    gnuan
size    30 80
cap     GNU Chess's analyser: give it a file of moves and a search depth and
cap     it annotates the game move by move, scoring each and naming the best
cap     line it found.  Moves it recognises from the opening book are marked
cap     `book'.  At the end of the file it prints the position reached and
cap     stops with `Bad move', which is how it reads end of input.
try     gnuan
run     mkdir -p /dd/tmp/GAMESA > /nil 2>/nil; echo "e2e4 e7e5 g1f3 b8c6 f1b5 a7a6" > /dd/tmp/GAMESA/moves
wait    4
run     builtin cd /dd
wait    1
run     clear
wait    1
run     gnuan
wait    8
send    tmp/GAMESA/moves\r
wait    5
send    2\r
wait    4
send    1\r
wait    30

shot    gnuchess
size    24 80
cap     GNU Chess after 1. e4, answered with the Sicilian (c7c5) straight
cap     out of the opening book, which ships here, read by the build
cap     `gnuchess' runs.  It asks for a
cap     time control first (`1' is a move a minute) and takes moves as
cap     `e2e4'; a clock for each side and the search depth sit to the right.
cap     It reads the terminal description out of TERMCAP itself, so the
cap     entry is put in the variable first.
try     gnuchess
run     . /dd/SYS/termcap.entry
wait    4
run     clear
wait    1
run     /dd/CMDS/gnuchess
wait    12
send    1\r
wait    10
send    e2e4\r
wait    22
kill

shot    gnuchessc
cap     GNU Chess 4.0 built for a curses display.  Its display files are
cap     compiled to a path this disk does not carry, so it draws no board;
cap     it still plays, taking a move as `e2e4' and printing its own reply
cap     on the next line.  For a board, see `gnuchess' or `nchess'.
try     gnuchessc
run     gnuchessc
wait    8
send    e2e4\r
wait    12

shot    gnuchessn
cap     A GNU Chess display that draws the squares as blocks of hashes, so
cap     the light and dark ones can be told apart on a terminal with no
cap     highlighting.  Moves go in as `e2e4'.  It reads
cap     TERMCAP as the terminal description itself, so the entry is put in
cap     the variable first.
try     gnuchessn
run     . /dd/SYS/termcap.entry
wait    4
run     clear
wait    1
run     gnuchessn
wait    14
send    e2e4\r
wait    15
kill

shot    gnuchessr
cap     GNU Chess with the plainest display of all -- pieces as letters,
cap     capitals for one side and lower case for the other, nothing that
cap     needs a terminal to draw.  It asks for the time control as
cap     `#moves #minutes' and then takes moves as `e2e4'.
try     gnuchessr
run     gnuchessr
wait    10
send    e2e4\r
wait    12

shot    mille
cap     Mille Bornes, the French road-race card game: your hand on the left
cap     numbered 1 to 6, the deck and the discard beside it, the scoring
cap     table on the right and the commands under it -- `p' picks, `u #'
cap     plays a card, `d #' discards, `s' saves the game and `q' quits.
try     mille
run     mille
wait    10
snap
send    \003
wait    3

shot    napoleon
size    24 80
cap     The opening: Napoleon states its licence, then describes the central
cap     office where the game begins -- the ebony desk, the chair you can twirl
cap     on, the one exit south -- and notes the scroll lying on the desk.  That
cap     scroll is where the licence it just mentioned can be read in full.  The
cap     `(napoleon)' prompt takes whole sentences, not verb-noun pairs.
try     napoleon
run     clear
wait    1
run     napoleon
wait    8
snap
send    quit\r
wait    3
send    q\r
wait    2

shot    nchess
cap     GNU Chess 4.0 with its plain display, thinking: the board in
cap     letters, then a line for each depth it searched showing the score,
cap     the time, the nodes and the line it likes, and the totals at the
cap     foot.  It asks for a time control as `#moves #minutes' first.
try     nchess
run     nchess
wait    8
send    e2e4\r
wait    12

shot    poker
cap     Cold-hand poker: it asks what you want to bet, deals, and lets you
cap     draw.  It wants the word `no', not `N', and says so in its own
cap     voice if you try.  One of four games written in SNOBOL4; their data
cap     is in GAMES/SNOBOL.
try     poker
run     poker
wait    5
send    no\r
wait    5
send    50\r
wait    6
send    10\r
wait    8

shot    queens
cap     The eight-queens problem for any board: it reads the size as a
cap     number on standard input and draws every arrangement it finds with
cap     no two queens attacking.  A six-by-six board here.
try     echo 6 | queens
os9     echo 6 ! queens
run     echo 6 | queens | head -n 21
wait    8

shot    tttt
cap     Tic-tac-toe on a four-by-four board, two moves in.  Name a square by
cap     its column letter and row digit -- `b1' -- and the machine answers.
try     tttt
run     tttt
wait    4
send    b1\r
wait    3
send    c2\r
wait    5
snap
send    q
wait    2

# --------------------------------------------------------- chess utilities

shot    bincheckr
cap     Checks a GNU Chess opening book for how well it is packed: entry
cap     size, book size, pocket and count.  Given the 145K book that ships
cap     in GAMES it reports the entry size and then zeroes for the rest, and
cap     is aborted as it closes the file.
try     bincheckr gnuchess.book
run     builtin cd /dd/GAMES
wait    1
run     clear
wait    1
run     bincheckr gnuchess.book
wait    10

shot    checkgame
size    30 80
cap     Reads a saved GNU Chess game -- the `chess.lst' that gnuchess writes
cap     when you type `list' -- replays it looking for illegal moves and
cap     prints the board it finishes on.  A game is made here first by
cap     playing one move against gnuchess and listing it.
try     checkgame chess.lst
run     mkdir -p /dd/tmp/GAMESA > /nil 2>/nil; rm -f /dd/tmp/GAMESA/chess.lst
run     . /dd/SYS/termcap.entry
run     load /dd/CMDS/GAMES/gnuchess
run     builtin cd /dd/tmp/GAMESA
wait    3
run     gnuchess > /nil 2>/nil
wait    12
send    1\r
wait    8
send    e2e4\r
wait    20
send    list\r
wait    8
send    quit\r
wait    6
run     clear
wait    1
run     cat chess.lst
wait    5
run     checkgame chess.lst
wait    10

shot    game
size    24 80
cap     Meant to draw a saved GNU Chess game board by board as PostScript,
cap     one page a move, for printing with the ChessFont file its
cap     documentation names.  Given a game it writes the first line of the
cap     page setup and stops there, so there is nothing to print.
cap     DOC/game has the manual page.
try     game g.lst 1 2
run     mkdir -p /dd/tmp/GAMESA > /nil 2>/nil; echo "e2e4      0      0      0     8" > /dd/tmp/GAMESA/g.lst
wait    4
run     builtin cd /dd/tmp/GAMESA
wait    1
run     clear
wait    1
run     game g.lst 1 2
wait    12

shot    postprint
cap     Meant to print the positions in GNU Chess's saved hash file as
cap     PostScript, a board to a page with the best move and the search
cap     depth.  Given GNU Chess's opening book in place of a saved hash it
cap     writes the first line of the page setup and stops.  DOC/postprint
cap     has the manual page.
try     postprint gnuchess.book
run     builtin cd /dd/GAMES
wait    1
run     clear
wait    1
run     postprint gnuchess.book
wait    12

# ------------------------------------------------------------ dungeon crawl

shot    hackwish
size    24 80
cap     The moment it wins -- the wish made, the game saved.  `Found in game N'
cap     counts the hacks it played before a wizard turned up holding a wand of
cap     wishing.
cap     It carries hack's full path built in, because hack reopens itself by
cap     the name it was launched with and bash passes only the bare word.
try     hackwish
run     hackwish
wait    75
snap
send    \003
wait    2

shot    hack
cap     Hack, the dungeon crawl NetHack grew out of: a room drawn in line
cap     characters, `@' for you, a letter for each monster -- `d' is a dog
cap     -- and your hit points, armour class, strength and experience along
cap     the foot.  It relaunches itself by the name it was started with, so
cap     the two shells want it differently: at bash give the whole pathlist
cap     shown, because bash hands a program only the bare word you typed; at
cap     the OS-9 shell plain `hack' is enough.  Its saves and scores live in
cap     GAMES/HACK/PLAYGROUND.
try     /dd/CMDS/GAMES/hack
os9     hack
run     /dd/CMDS/GAMES/hack
wait    10
send    n\r
wait    6
snap
send    \003
wait    3

shot    nethack3
size    24 80
cap     A new character on the first level of the Mazes of Menace: the room,
cap     the `@' that is you and a pet beside you, and the two status lines --
cap     strength, dexterity and the rest, then dungeon level, gold, hit points,
cap     power, armour class and experience.  `y' at the first question lets it
cap     choose your character; `?' lists the commands, `S' saves, `Q' quits.
try     nethack3
run     export TERM=vt100
wait    1
run     clear
wait    1
run     nethack3
wait    10
send    y
wait    8
send    \s
wait    4
send    \s
wait    4
snap
send    Q
wait    3
send    y
wait    4
send    n
wait    6

shot    moria
size    24 80
cap     A new warrior in the town at the top of the dungeon: the walls of the
cap     six shops, numbered by their doors, the `@' that is you, and down the
cap     left the character's stats, hit points and gold.  `?' lists the
cap     commands, `^X' saves and `^K' quits.
try     moria
run     export TERM=vt100 USER=tester
wait    1
run     clear
wait    1
run     moria
wait    12
send    \s
wait    5
send    a
wait    4
send    m
wait    4
send    \e
wait    4
send    a
wait    5
send    Tester\r
wait    8
send    \s
wait    10
snap
send    \013
wait    4
send    y
wait    8

shot    mw
size    24 80
cap     The maze from above, and along the bottom the score strip for eight
cap     players.  A computer player, started with `mw -l=5' before this game,
cap     has joined as player 2.  `w' walks, `a' and `d' turn, `s' shoots,
cap     `m' drops a mine and `Q' quits.
try     mw
run     export TERM=vt100 USER=tester
run     mw -l=5 >/nil &
wait    3
run     clear
wait    1
run     mw
wait    14
send    d
wait    3
send    s
wait    6
snap
send    Q
wait    5

shot    trek
size    24 80
cap     A short game at novice skill, and the short-range scan of the first
cap     quadrant: the Enterprise is E, a starbase #, stars *, and beside the
cap     grid the ship's stardate, condition, energy, torpedoes and shields.
cap     `help' lists the commands; `terminate' ends the game.
try     trek
run     export TERM=vt100
wait    1
run     clear
wait    1
run     trek
wait    4
send    \r
wait    2
send    s\r
wait    2
send    n\r
wait    2
send    x\r
wait    4
send    s\r
wait    4
snap
send    terminate\r
wait    2
send    n\r
wait    3

shot    atc
size    24 80
cap     The easy airport a few seconds in: the radar with its exits round the
cap     edge, airport 0 and a beacon, and the first plane as its letter and
cap     altitude.  Beside it the clock, the count of planes brought in safely,
cap     and each plane with where it is bound.  Talk to a plane by its letter;
cap     `?' lists what may come next, ^C quits.
try     atc -g easy
run     export TERM=vt100 USER=tester
wait    1
run     clear
wait    1
run     atc -g easy
wait    14
snap
send    \003
wait    3
send    y
wait    6

shot    bandit
size    24 80
cap     A pull of the one-armed bandit: the three reels after the spin, the
cap     payoff table beside them, and the bankroll and bet prompt below.  It
cap     reads TERMCAP as the terminal description itself, so the entry is put
cap     in the variable first.  `q' at the bet prompt walks away with the total.
try     bandit
run     . /dd/SYS/termcap.entry
wait    2
run     clear
wait    1
run     bandit
wait    5
send    n\r
wait    4
send    5\r
wait    5
snap
send    q\r
wait    4

shot    typefast
size    24 80
cap     A few seconds into an easy game: the words drift down toward the
cap     bottom border, and the hits and misses so far sit at the top.  Type a
cap     word and SPACE before it lands.  It reads TERMCAP as the terminal
cap     description itself, so the entry is put in the variable first.
try     typefast
run     . /dd/SYS/termcap.entry
wait    2
run     clear
wait    1
run     typefast
wait    5
send    n
wait    3
send    1
