# -------------------------------------------------------------- other games

shot    fuddle
size    24 80
cap     A chess variant that shuffles -- "fuddles" -- the pieces into fresh
cap     places and plays a game from there.  It asks whether you want white,
cap     draws a shaded board with a compact list of the pieces above it, makes
cap     its own move, and waits for yours, taken as two squares like `e2e4'.
cap     It addresses the terminal the TeleVideo way, so the board is redrawn a
cap     character at a time; here it is White's move.
try     fuddle
run     fuddle
wait    6
send    y\r
wait    14
snap

shot    hotel
cap     hotel, a two-player board game played by coordinates.  It asks how
cap     many are playing and then draws the board.
try     hotel
run     hotel
wait    5
send    N\r
wait    4
send    2\r
wait    5
send    Alice\r
wait    4
send    Bob\r
wait    8

shot    stone
cap     A stones game in the Nim family.  You set the size of the pile, the
cap     winning parity and the most that may be taken at once, then you and
cap     the machine take turns removing stones.  Here the pile is 21 and it
cap     is asking for your first take.  One of four games written in SNOBOL4;
cap     their data is in GAMES/SNOBOL.
try     stone
run     stone
wait    5
send    no\r
wait    5
send    21\r
wait    6
send    1\r
wait    6
send    3\r
wait    8

shot    draw
for     draw
cap     A character-graphics drawing program: it rules a canvas across the
cap     terminal and you move a cursor over it laying down characters.  This
cap     is what `?' shows -- vi's hjkl to move, yubn for
cap     the diagonals, `p' to lift and drop the pen, backslash to choose the
cap     character it draws with, and a shell escape.  The keys can be
cap     rebound through an environment variable.
try     draw
run     draw
wait    8
keys    ?
wait    6
kill

shot    lorenz3d
for     lorenz3d
cap     The Lorenz attractor in three dimensions, asked for parameter by
cap     parameter: the time step, the z magnification and offset, then
cap     Lorenz's own a, b and c -- 10, 28 and 8/3 here, the values that
cap     make the butterfly.  What it draws with is Tektronix vector codes,
cap     so on a vt100 the picture arrives as characters; the dialogue is
cap     the part worth capturing.
try     lorenz3d
run     lorenz3d
wait    4
send    0.01\r
wait    2
send    20\r
wait    2
send    0\r
wait    2
send    10\r
wait    2
send    28\r
wait    2
send    2.667\r
wait    2
kill


# The rest of "Other games", one stanza each.

shot    tt
cap     Tetris for terminals, a few pieces in: , and / move a piece, .
cap     rotates it, space drops it, s pauses and q quits.
try     tt
run     tt
wait    4
keys    ,,.
wait    2
send    \s
wait    3
keys    //
wait    2
send    \s
wait    3
snap
send    q
wait    2

shot    zot
cap     Prints a line of text in one of fourteen animated styles: the
cap     letters slide in from a side, bounce, sort themselves, or turn up
cap     one at a time like a game-show board, and settle as the line.  Shown
cap     as a strip, one line per frame, top to bottom: Wheel of Fortune
cap     (style 14), then Expand text outward (style 5).  zot -s names all
cap     fourteen.
try     zot -s=14 "Microware OS-9/68000 -- the freeware collection, 1987 to 1996"
frames
run     zot -s=14 "Microware OS-9/68000 -- the freeware collection, 1987 to 1996"
wait    6
run     zot -s=5 "Microware OS-9/68000 -- the freeware collection, 1987 to 1996"
wait    6

shot    convert
cap     Starts the WORLD text adventure.  Its opening sets you on a trail
cap     near the top of a pass on a strange world, your landing craft and the
cap     dome that houses the rest of your party far below, and leaves you at
cap     the `>>>' prompt.
try     convert
run     convert
wait    5
send    \r
wait    6
snap
send    \r
wait    4
snap
send    quit\r
wait    3

shot    vtxtcn
cap     A build step for the World adventure: it packs the game's text from
cap     vtext.dat, in the current directory, into q1text.dat and writes the
cap     qtext, objdes and gtext tables as .inc files for the compile.  It
cap     reports the packed and unpacked sizes.  vtext.dat ships in SRC/world.
try     vtxtcn
run     rm -rf /dd/tmp/VTXT > /nil 2>/nil; mkdir -p /dd/tmp/VTXT; cp /dd/SRC/world/vtext.dat /dd/tmp/VTXT/vtext.dat
wait    3
run     builtin cd /dd/tmp/VTXT
wait    1
run     clear
wait    1
run     vtxtcn; ls -l
wait    20

shot    mkindex
cap     Builds the index bog reads its dictionary through, in the
cap     directory holding the dictionary; it reports two counts.
try     mkindex
run     builtin cd /dd/GAMES/BOG
wait    1
run     clear
wait    1
run     mkindex < /nil
wait    12
run     ls -l
wait    3

shot    mkdict
cap     Builds bog's dictionary from a word list, run in the directory that
cap     holds the list.  It works quietly and leaves the dictionary and its
cap     help text in GAMES/BOG, where bog reads them.
try     mkdict
run     builtin cd /dd/GAMES/BOG
wait    1
run     clear
wait    1
run     mkdict < /nil
wait    12
run     ls -l
wait    5

shot    wisecrack
cap     A server: started in the background with the `&', it feeds slogans
cap     from a German OS-9 club seminar of 1992-93 into /pipe/txtpipe, in
cap     German -- "VIEL SPASS MIT OSK" is "have fun with OSK" -- and prints
cap     nothing itself.  Each `ask' then pulls out the next one.  Type the
cap     `&': started in the foreground it waits for a reader and, under
cap     os9exec, neither Ctrl-C nor Ctrl-E gets the terminal back.  Stop it
cap     when you have finished with it -- `signal <id> 0', the id bash
cap     printed in angle brackets -- because a server left parked on a read
cap     after its shell has gone is the one thing here that can take the
cap     emulator with it.
try     wisecrack &
run     wisecrack &
wait    3
run     ask
wait    5
run     ask
wait    5
run     signal 3 0
wait    3

shot    ask
cap     Reads the next message from the pipe wisecrack writes to and
cap     prints it, one a call, in German: they are the slogans of a 1992-93
cap     OS-9 club seminar.  wisecrack must be running behind it, started
cap     with the `&' as shown; on its own ask says "No Wisecracks coming".
cap     Stop the server when you have finished -- `signal <id> 0', the id
cap     bash printed -- because one left parked on the pipe after its shell
cap     has gone can take the emulator with it.
try     wisecrack &; ask
run     wisecrack &
wait    3
run     ask
wait    5
run     ask
wait    5
run     signal 3 0
wait    3

shot    shuffle
cap     A switch puzzle.  A row of numbered switches is wired together so
cap     that flipping one flips the switches linked to it; you choose a
cap     switch by number at the `Wich switch ?' prompt, and the aim is to
cap     line them all up.  The level and your move count are along the foot;
cap     `q' quits.
try     shuffle
run     shuffle
wait    6
snap
send    q
wait    2

